  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "text": "about:blank is the deliberate synchronous popup reservation. The public coordinator then lazy-loads its runtime, which adopts the handle and replaces its location with the same-origin auth start URL. Blank initial state alone is not a failure."
    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "text": "Auth start path is /api/auth/cinaauth/login. Default intent is portal and callback is /account. Popup mode adds a correlation UUID. Account registration mode adds mode=register; this is an account prompt, not dynamic OIDC client registration."
    },
    {
      "id": "F3",
      "text": "The authorization builder sets client_id, redirect_uri, response_type=code, scope=openid profile email, resource=appOrigin, state, nonce, code_challenge and code_challenge_method=S256. It sets no prompt=none. Only login mode=register adds prompt=create."
    },
    {
      "id": "F4",
      "text": "Issuer/account/app origins require exact canonical HTTPS origins, no credentials, port, path suffix, query or fragment. Client ID and nonempty required-role list are checked. The defaults below are source defaults only and are not proven deployed values."
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "text": "The login route requires all three OIDC client/bridge/transaction secrets at minimum32 characters before discovery and authorize redirect. It uses optional CINAAUTH_AUTH_SERVICE binding for server-side discovery, otherwise server fetch. Discovery/issuer metadata is not visible in browser network events."
    },
    {
      "id": "F6",
      "text": "A login exception returns same-origin popup completion HTML with oidc_unavailable and HTTP200, or a nonpopup302 error redirect. The newly observed login302 plus actual issuer navigation rules out this direct-unavailable shape for that observation; it does not prove every runtime configuration value or server-side step."
    },
    {
      "id": "F7",
      "text": "The callback reads config/secrets and the state-matched sealed transaction, rediscovers issuer metadata, then calls authorization-code exchange including oauth.validateAuthResponse. An invalid transaction has no recovered popup handle and uses a302 fallback. A caught callback failure is collapsed to oidc_failed; with a recovered popup transaction it returns200 completion. Success also returns200 popup completion. HTTP200 alone cannot distinguish those two results."
    },
    {
      "id": "F8",
      "text": "Completion HTML writes same-origin storage, posts a message to the app origin and broadcasts the result, then schedules window.close after75ms and a local callback fallback after750ms. This is compatible with closure approximately108ms after callback; it does not establish which callback failure occurred."
    },
    {
      "id": "F9",
      "text": "The client handles explicit failed completion, verification failures, timeout or navigation failure by releasing and closing the reserved popup. The message transport verifies same-origin and the expected popup handle; it does not use popup.closed to infer success because of COOP. No browser console error is required for these handled failures."
    }
  ],
